# fixedbugs, M-c-callbacks step 0, 2026-09-05 (panel 111 R4).
#
# SYMPTOM: the first correct callback binding ever written was exit 2 —
# `internal error: compiling the generated C failed`, with clang saying `unknown
# type name 'h_0fn_294870dd'` on the probe line, under a `#line` pointing at
# this declaration. The compiler blamed itself for a program that had nothing
# wrong with it, which is the failure §4.19's guarantee exists to prevent.
#
# CAUSE: two of them, and both were invisible until a boundary type needed a
# typedef. (1) A per-module TU carries only the typedefs its own reach names,
# and an `extern` is in neither of the two lists that build that reach — it is
# nobody's Heroes function, so its parameter types never joined. (2) Even once
# they did, the probe was emitted in the PRELUDE, which runs before the typedef
# section, against emit.hero's own written invariant "types before anything that
# can mention one". Every earlier boundary type spells itself without a table —
# `int64_t`, `HeroStr`, `void *` — so neither gap could fire.
#
# FIX: `emit/unit.hero` joins every extern's declared parameter types to the
# reach, asked of the same `tu_p` the prelude writes probes from; and both
# emitters call `extern_probes` after `typeorder.definitions` instead of inside
# the prelude. The emit goldens moved by exactly that block and by nothing else,
# measured: the same multiset of lines and the same line count.

extern "stdlib.h"
    function atexit(f: (function() -> ())) -> i32

function bye()
    print("bye")

function main()
    if atexit(bye) != 0
        print("atexit refused the handler")
